Below is a small, working skeleton for a Python library that can be turned into an installable package in seconds.  
The example uses pyproject.toml + setuptools, so you don’t need any third‑party build tools (e.g. Poetry) and it will work out‑of‑the‑box on most systems.

Feel free to copy‑paste the blocks directly into your terminal or edit them in an editor of your choice.


1️⃣ Directory layout


my_pkg/
├─ pyproject.toml
├─ README.md            ← package description (recommended)
├─ LICENSE              ← choose a license e.g. MIT
├─ src/
│   └─ my_pkg/
│       ├─ init.py  ← public API
│       └─ core.py      ← stub implementation
└─ tests/
    └─ test_core.py     ← unit tests



2️⃣ Create the scaffold

bash
create top‑level package directory and subdirs
mkdir -p my_pkg/src/my_pkg
mkdir -p my_pkg/tests

minimal README & LICENSE (choose MIT/Apache or your own)
tee my_pkg/README.md > /dev/null <<'EOF'
my_pkg

A short description of what the library does.

Example usage:

python
from my_pkg import foo

print(foo())

EOF

tee my_pkg/LICENSE > /dev/null <<'EOF'
MIT License
Copyright (c) $(date +%Y)
...
EOF



3️⃣ pyproject.toml – a fully PEP‑517 compatible build file

bash
tee my_pkg/pyproject.toml > /dev/null <<'EOF'
[build-system]
requires = ["setuptools>=61.0", "wheel"]
build-backend = "setuptools.build_meta"

[project]
name = "my-pkg"
version = "0.1.0"
description = "A minimal example package"
readme = "README.md"
license = {file="LICENSE"}
authors = [{name="Your Name", email="you@example.com"}]

keywords = ["example", "demo"]
requires-python = ">=3.9"

classifiers=[
    "Programming Language :: Python :: 3",
    "License :: OSI Approved :: MIT License",
    "Operating System :: OS Independent",
]

dependencies=[]            # add runtime deps here
[project.optional-dependencies]
dev = ["pytest"]           # dev helpers

[tool.setuptools.packages.find]
where = ["src"]
exclude = ["tests*"]

[tool.setuptools.package-data]
my_pkg = ["*.txt"]

Optional: entry points for console scripts
[project.scripts]
my-pkg-cli = "my_pkg.cli:main"
EOF



4️⃣ Minimal package code

src/my_pkg/init.py

python
from .core import foo  # re‑export public function


src/my_pkg/core.py

python
def foo() -> str:
    """Return a greeting."""
    return "Hello from my-pkg!"



5️⃣ Unit test

tests/test_core.py

python
import pytest
from my_pkg import foo

def test_foo():
    assert foo() == "Hello from my-pkg!"



6️⃣ Build & install locally

bash
cd my_pkg
build wheel and source distribution
python -m build   # this uses pyproject.toml
install the built package into your current venv
pip install dist/*.whl


Test that it works:

bash
python - <<'PY'
import my_pkg
print(my_pkg.foo())
PY


You should see: Hello from my-pkg!


7️⃣ (Optional) Add a CLI entry point

If you want a simple command‑line tool, create src/my_pkg/cli.py:

python
#!/usr/bin/env python3
import sys
from .core import foo

def main(argv=None):
    print(foo())

if name == "main":
    main()


Then rebuild (python -m build) and you can run it with:

bash
my-pkg-cli
outputs: Hello from my-pkg!



